course: religion-ethics
lesson: 21
title: What Christians believe: God, Jesus and the Holy Spirit
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Christians believe in one God who made everything out of love and keeps it going, like a singer who keeps the song alive by singing it.
explain: They believe this one God is known in three persons: the Father, the Son (Jesus) and the Holy Spirit; this is called the Trinity, and Christians admit it is a mystery too big to explain fully.
explain: Jesus taught his friends to call God «Father», even «Abba», a warm family word, so prayer is like talking to a perfect parent.
explain: Christians believe Jesus is both fully God and fully human, that his name means «God saves», and that «Christ» is a title, not a surname: it means the anointed one, the promised Messiah.
explain: The Holy Spirit is God with people here and now, giving comfort, courage and guidance; the Bible and the short creeds said in church gather these beliefs together.
explain: For a child today this means that God is not far away or angry by default: the Christian picture is of a God who is love and who wants a friendship with every person.
fact: Christians believe there is one God who made everything and keeps it in being.
fact: The First Letter of John in the Bible says «God is love» (1 John 4:8).
fact: The Trinity means one God in three persons: the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit.
fact: Pictures like a shamrock (a little clover with three leaves on one stem), or water as ice, liquid and steam, help explain the Trinity, but every picture falls short.
fact: Jesus called God «Father», and sometimes «Abba», a loving family word in his language.
fact: Christians believe Jesus is both fully God and fully human.
fact: The name Jesus means «God saves»; Christians call him the Saviour who rescues people from sin.
fact: «Christ» is a title meaning «the anointed one», the Messiah promised in the Jewish Scriptures.
fact: Jews do not accept Jesus as the Messiah; Muslims honour him as a great prophet but not as God's Son.
fact: Christians believe the Holy Spirit is God present with and in believers; wind, fire and a dove are pictures of the Spirit.
fact: Most Protestant Bibles have 66 books; Catholic and Orthodox Bibles have a few more, because they keep some extra older Jewish books that Protestants left out.
fact: How literally to read each part of the Bible is disputed among Christians.
fact: The Apostles' Creed and the Nicene Creed are short statements of belief said together in church.
fact: A few groups that call themselves Christian do not accept the Trinity.
exercise: What do Christians believe about who made the world? | One God made everything and keeps it in being | The world made itself and God came later | Many different gods each made a different part of the world | Angels made the world while God rested
exercise: Which Bible verse sums up the Christian idea of God in three words? | God is love | God is anger | God is far | God is silent
exercise: What is the Trinity? | One God in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit | Three different gods who work as a team | God, Mary and Jesus as a holy family | Three angels who stand before God's throne
exercise: Why do Sunday-school teachers use a shamrock to talk about the Trinity? | It has three leaves but is one plant | It grows in Ireland where Jesus lived | It is lucky, so it brings God near | It has four leaves, one for each gospel
exercise: Where do pictures like water, ice and steam fall short for the Trinity? | No picture can fully show the mystery of God | They show far too many persons in God, more than three | They prove God is made of water | They work perfectly, nothing is missing
exercise: What word did Jesus use for God that shows a close, loving family bond? | Abba, meaning Father | Rabbi, meaning teacher | Amen, meaning so be it | Hosanna, meaning save us
exercise: What do Christians believe about who Jesus is? | Fully God and fully human at the same time | Only a good man who taught well and helped people | Only God pretending to be a man for a short while | Half God and half man, like a hero in an old story
exercise: What does the name Jesus mean? | God saves | God is king | Son of Mary | Light of the world
exercise: Is «Christ» Jesus' surname? | No, it is a title meaning the anointed one | Yes, it was his family's surname back in Nazareth | Yes, his father Joseph was called Christ | No, it is the name of his home town
exercise: How do Jews see Jesus, put fairly? | They do not accept him as the promised Messiah | They believe he is the Son of God too | They have never heard of him at all | They think he is the third person of God, the Spirit
exercise: Who do Christians believe the Holy Spirit is? | God present with believers, giving comfort and courage | A ghost of Jesus that visits churches | An angel sent to guard each person | A feeling people get when they sing hymns
exercise: Which three pictures does the Bible use for the Holy Spirit? | Wind, fire and a dove | Rock, sword and lion | Water, bread and wine | Star, crown and lamb
exercise: How many books are in most Protestant Bibles? | 66 | 73 | 12 | 100
exercise: Why do Catholic and Orthodox Bibles have more books? | They include extra older Jewish books Protestants left out | Their monks wrote new books last century | They count every psalm as a separate book | They add the lives of the saints at the end
exercise: What is a creed? | A short statement of shared Christian belief | A long list of church rules and fines for breaking them | A song sung by the whole choir only at Christmas | A prayer said only by the priest, never by the people
exercise: Name two creeds said in church. | The Apostles' Creed and the Nicene Creed | The Lord's Prayer and the Hail Mary | The Ten Commandments and the Beatitudes of Jesus | Psalm 23 and the Magnificat
exercise: What does «Jesus the Saviour» mean to Christians? | Jesus rescues people from sin and brings them back to God | Jesus saves money for the church's poor | Jesus saved Israel from the Roman army | Jesus saved the disciples from a storm only
test: my little bro says the Trinity means christians have 3 gods lol. is he right? | No, Christians believe in one God in three persons | Yes, Father, Son and Spirit are three gods | Yes, but they only pray to the strongest one | No, Christians believe in two gods and one angel above them
test: Ayesha, a Muslim classmate, asks: «Do you think we Muslims hate Jesus?» What is the fair answer? | No, Muslims honour Jesus as a great prophet | Yes, Muslims refuse to mention Jesus at all | No, Muslims believe Jesus is God's Son too | Yes, Muslims think Jesus never existed
test: «Jesus Christ, so his mum was Mrs Christ, right?» says your cousin. Put him right. | Christ is a title meaning the anointed one | Christ was Mary's name before she married | Christ was the name of their village near Nazareth | Christ was a nickname his friends made up
test: Sam, who goes to no church, asks: «If God's so great, why do you lot say you can't explain him?» Best reply? | Christians believe God is bigger than any words can hold | Christians made God up, so they forgot the details | Only priests are allowed to explain God | The Bible forbids anyone to talk about God
test: «Abba? Isn't that a band?» laughs a kid in class. What did Jesus mean by it? | A warm family word for Father | A Greek word meaning king | A Hebrew word meaning so be it | A name for the Holy Spirit
test: Your sister says the dove painted in church means the Holy Spirit is a bird. Put her right. | The dove is a picture of God's Spirit, not a real bird | Yes, the Spirit lives in doves near churches | The dove means peace only, nothing about God | The dove is there because Noah built the church
test: «Shut up, Jesus was just a nice bloke who told stories,» says an older boy. What do Christians believe instead? | Jesus is fully God and fully human | Jesus was an angel who looked like a man | Jesus was a king who later became God | Jesus was God and only seemed human
test: Your friend from a Jewish family says Jesus isn't the Messiah for her. How do you answer respectfully? | That is a real difference; Christians believe he is | She must be wrong, because the creeds of the church say so | She is not allowed to say that at school | It does not matter, they are the same faith
test: An olympiad judge asks: «Why say a creed together, not just believe it quietly?» Best answer? | It joins Christians across churches in one shared faith | The words only work if said very loudly | It is a test to see which people have forgotten the words | Saying it aloud makes God hear better
test: «My Bible has 73 books and yours has 66, so one's a fake!» says a Catholic friend, half joking. What's true? | Both are real; churches accept a few books differently | The Catholic Bible is fake, it has extra pages added by monks | The Protestant Bible is missing torn-out pages | Both are fake, the real one has 100 books
test: A judge follows up: «Do all Christians read every part of the Bible word for word?» | No, how literally to read it is disputed | Yes, every Christian reads it all literally | No, Christians ignore the Old Testament | Yes, it is a rule in every single church
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Christian doctrine of the Trinity? | One God subsisting in three persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit | Three distinct deities acting in concert | A single person appearing successively in three disguises | God, the Virgin Mary and Jesus as a divine household
exam: Identify the signification of the title «Christ» as applied to Jesus of Nazareth. | The anointed one, the Messiah promised in the Jewish Scriptures | The family name borne by Joseph of Nazareth | The designation of the town wherein Jesus was born | A title conferred upon Jesus by the Roman authorities at his trial
exam: Select the response consistent with Christian teaching concerning the nature of Jesus. | That he is at once fully divine and fully human | That he is solely human and possessed no divinity | That he is solely divine and merely appeared human | That he became divine only subsequent to his death
exam: State the number of books comprised in the Bible as generally received by Protestant churches. | Sixty-six | Seventy-three | Twelve | One hundred
